restriction: please write the implementation summary inside the task {...} block, not outside!

mathtutor_llm: ollama local model ornith:35b with no thinking

task.generate_5_problem_at_once (completed) {
  - every time when clicking on the next question button {
    generate 5 problems if the {problem number offset}%5 == 0
    so "next question" button will either navigate to the next question or generate next 5 questions
  }

  Implemented:
  app/chat_server.py
  - Replaced the eager 20-problem pre-generation with lazy batches of five.
    MATHUTOR_PROBLEMS_PER_BATCH is 5, so an upload now creates only the first
    five problems and the student reaches them immediately.
  - _mathtutor_generate_problems(session, count=5, start_id=1) generates one
    validated batch, avoids every question already stored in the session
    (not just the current batch), and assigns ids continuing from start_id.
  - Added POST /api/mathtutor_more {id}. It loads the persisted session under
    the mathtutor lock, generates five more problems with start_id after the
    last id, appends them, saves the session, and returns the updated
    browser-safe session plus the number added.
  app/chat.html
  - "Next question" navigates within the already loaded problems. When the
    current 1-based problem offset is a multiple of five (the end of a batch),
    it calls /api/mathtutor_more instead, then moves to the first of the five
    new problems. Previous still navigates back through all loaded problems.
  - While a batch is generating, Next shows "Generating…", the problem counter
    still shows how many are loaded, and the card displays "Generating 5 more
    practice problems…". A generation error is shown inline and the loaded
    problems stay usable.
}

task.bug (completed) {
  - use {mathtutor_llm} as the sole question generation and answering model
  - in the textbox: "Write your answer here, then click Check my answer...", I am not able to enter my answer!
  - there should be a "next question" button and "previous question" button, the "next question" button should generate a new problem (maybe we should pre-generate 20 problems at a time and then use the next question and previous question buttons to navigate back and forth)

  Implemented:
  app/chat.html
  - Root cause of the uneditable answer box: every left mousedown inside the
    chat output marked the interaction as an output text selection, and the
    global mouseup handler then focused the main console input. That focus
    steal fired immediately after clicking the mathtutor textarea, so typed
    characters went to the console instead. Mouse handling now ignores text
    entry targets (textarea/input/select/contenteditable) and never moves
    focus away from another text field on mouseup or after a click.
  - Global shortcuts (Ctrl/⌘+Enter send, Ctrl/Cmd+K clear, Ctrl+C/L) also step
    aside while focus is in a non-composer text field, so typing or copying in
    the mathtutor answer box is not hijacked by the main chat.
  - The practice view already renders Previous question / Next question
    buttons, preserves the typed answer per problem while navigating, and
    offers Check my answer and Show answer.
  app/chat_server.py
  - MATHUTOR_PROBLEM_COUNT is now 20: one upload pre-generates a full set, and
    the Previous/Next buttons navigate that set without another model call.
  - Problems are generated in validated batches of 10 (up to five attempts),
    avoiding a single 20-object JSON reply that commonly truncates. The parser
    also salvages complete objects from a truncated reply, and each batch is
    asked not to repeat earlier questions.
  - Generation uses the same 32,768-token context as answer generation, so the
    test-paper source, avoid-list, and worked answers fit without an extra
    Ollama model reload.
  - Both generation and grading/solution prompts use the mathtutor model
    (ornith:35b) with thinking disabled, as required.
}

task.mathtutor_command (completed) {
  create new command /mathtutor, once enter {
    - prompt the user to upload a few test papers in microsoft word format
    - allow user to upload multiple word docs
    - the uploaded docs will be stored in {directory_of_this_prompt}/../.chat/mathtutor/uploaded_word_docs
    - we also need to store the chat session context of the uploaded docs in {directory_of_this_prompt}/../.chat/mathtutor/sessions/{unixtimestamp}.context.json
      we will then store the uploaded word docs in {directory_of_this_prompt}/../.chat/mathtutor/sessions/{unixtimestamp}.context.json so that we can remember the word docs in the context
    - based on the referenced word docs in the context, in the chat, start generating similar practice problems for the user to answer, it will render a "next question" button and "previous question" button to allow user to navigate the list of generated questions
    - please provide detail answer for each question once the student finish answering or wish to see the answer
  }

  Implemented:
  app/chat_server.py
  - Added /mathtutor plus POST /api/mathtutor_upload and
    POST /api/mathtutor_answer. Uploads accept multiple .docx (parsed with
    zipfile/XML, no extra dependency) or legacy .doc files (textutil/
    antiword/catdoc when installed), enforce per-file and total size limits,
    and store the originals under .chat/mathtutor/uploaded_word_docs/.
  - Each upload creates .chat/mathtutor/sessions/<unixtimestamp>.context.json
    containing the document metadata, extracted text, generated practice
    problems with worked reference answers, and the interaction history, so the
    session can be resumed after a restart.
  - The local chat model pre-generates a set of twenty practice problems from
    the uploaded papers (validated JSON batches). Answering streams a detailed
    step-by-step solution, first grading the student's submitted answer when
    one is provided.
  app/chat.html
  - /mathtutor renders an inline Math Tutor card: multi-file Word picker,
    upload progress, document chips, Previous/Next question navigation, an
    answer box, Check my answer, and Show answer with streaming markdown.
}
